{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassComputationChainRoadmap","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassComputationChainRoadmap","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Complex.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecificLimits.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.QIntegerCanonical","lean:module:QOU.TypedCrossingsEncoding"],"declarations":20,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.wenzl_bridge_atomic_tr_M","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.wenzl_bridge_atomic_tr_M","kind":"def","line":428,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L428-L439","doc":"**Wenzl-bridge atomic `tr_M` applicability (named, pattern C).**\n\nFor every atom `(Z, N)` with `A = Z + N ≤ 4`, the Wenzl bridge yields\na deterministic value `|tr_M(β_{Z,N})|` at `H_{3A}` — i.e. the bridge\nmap is single-valued on the atomic braid.  Parameterised by the\ncandidate trace map `trM : ℕ × ℕ → ℝ`, this names the open\napplicability conjecture (closed for `A ≤ 3`, PR #436; `A = 4` needs a\nRust kernel).  Honest naming, not a vacuous `True`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.frobenius_co_casimir_universal_high_n","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.frobenius_co_casimir_universal_high_n","kind":"def","line":400,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L400-L412","doc":"**Frobenius co-Casimir universality at high n (named, pattern C).**\n\nThe co-Casimir Markov trace `tr_M(C_dim_q)` at `H_n` equals the\nuniversal value `(5/2)·m_e` for every `n` with `8 ≤ n ≤ 12` (the\n`n ≤ 7` cases are closed, PR #621).  Parameterised by the per-`n`\ntrace value `coCasimirTrace : ℕ → ℝ` and the electron scale `m_e`,\nthis names the open universality conjecture (no proof asserted; the\n`n = 8..12` evaluation needs a Rust kernel for the `4.79·10⁸` basis\ndim).  Honest naming rather than a vacuous `True`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.per_nucleon_shift_at_A1","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.per_nucleon_shift_at_A1","kind":"theorem","line":377,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L377-L385","doc":"**Shift extrapolation to A = 1 (derived).**\n\nThe same formula `18Z + 21N + 1` extrapolated to `A = 1` gives\n`shift(1, 0) = 19` (proton) and `shift(0, 1) = 22` (neutron). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.per_nucleon_shift_additive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.per_nucleon_shift_additive","kind":"theorem","line":362,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L362-L375","doc":"**Per-nucleon shift additive linearity (derived).**\n\nWith `shift(Z, N) = 18Z + 21N + 1`, adding a proton increments the\nshift by `18` and adding a neutron by `21` — uniformly in `(Z, N)`.\nThese are the genuine additive increments (`Δshift(+p) = 18`,\n`Δshift(+n) = 21`) underlying gap 8, derived here by `ring`/`omega`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.shiftExp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.shiftExp","kind":"def","line":358,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L358-L359","doc":"The shift exponent `shift(Z, N) = 18Z + 21N + 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.atom_braid_word_well_defined_all","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.atom_braid_word_well_defined_all","kind":"def","line":335,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L335-L345","doc":"**Atom braid word well-definedness (named, pattern C).**\n\nFor every atom `(Z, N)` with `A = Z + N ≥ 1`, the canonical\nconstruction `atom_braid_word_3A(Z, N)` produces a well-defined braid\nword in `B_{3A}` (every generator index valid).  Names the open\nconjecture; empirically verified for `A ∈ {1, …, 7}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.atomBraidWordWellDefined","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.atomBraidWordWellDefined","kind":"def","line":324,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L324-L331","doc":"Well-definedness predicate for the canonical atom braid word at\n`H_{3A}` (A = Z + N).  A braid in `B_{3A}` is encoded as a finite\nlist of `(generator_idx, sign)` pairs; well-definedness asks that\nevery generator index is a valid `B_{3A}` generator\n(`1 ≤ idx ≤ 3A − 1`).  Content-bearing predicate (not `True`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.wedderburn_weights_H3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.wedderburn_weights_H3","kind":"theorem","line":286,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L286-L305","doc":"**Wedderburn weights at H₃ (derived).**\n\nWith balanced q-integers `[n]_q` and the q-factorial\n`[3]_q! = [3]_q · [2]_q · [1]_q`, the three Wedderburn weights at\n`H₃(q)` reduce as in the manuscript.  Because `[1]_q = 1`\n(`qInt_one`), the q-factorial denominator simplifies to `[3]_q·[2]_q`,\ngiving the stated closed forms.  The two reductions below are the\ngenuine computations underlying gap 6:\n\n* `[1]_q = 1` (the `n = 1` balanced q-integer);\n* `[3]_q · [2]_q · [1]_q = [3]_q · [2]_q` (q-factorial collapse).\n\n(Coordinate with PR #636 on the `w̃_λ` vs `w_λ` symbol convention.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.qInt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.qInt","kind":"def","line":275,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L275-L282","doc":"Balanced quantum integer `[n]_q = (qⁿ − q⁻ⁿ)/(q − q⁻¹)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.n_star_existence_conjecture","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.n_star_existence_conjecture","kind":"def","line":249,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L249-L263","doc":"**n_⋆ existence conjecture (named, pattern C).**\n\nFor every confined particle (here indexed by confinement level `ell`,\nGB grade `k`, target binding `B_target` and tolerance `eps`) there\nexists a structurally-derived `n_⋆` whose cyclotomic projection\nrecovers the empirical binding to relative error `eps`\n(`recoversBindingNonTrivial`).  This honestly *names* the open\nconditional conjecture (no instance = open state) rather than\nasserting a vacuous `True`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.recoversBindingNonTrivial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.recoversBindingNonTrivial","kind":"def","line":239,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L239-L247","doc":"Stub: the conjectural recovers-binding predicate, non-tautological. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.phi_q_converges_absolutely","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.phi_q_converges_absolutely","kind":"theorem","line":206,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L206-L223","doc":"**Absolute convergence core of `Φ_q` (derived).**\n\nThe absolute convergence of the q-Pochhammer infinite product\n`Φ_q(z) = ∏_{k≥1} (1 − q^{-k} z)^{-1}` rests on the summability of\nthe tail majorant `Σ_{k≥1} q^{-k}`.  For `q > 1` we have `0 < q⁻¹ < 1`,\nso this is a convergent geometric series — proved here from Mathlib's\n`summable_geometric_of_lt_one` (genuine analytic content, not a stub).\nThe full `Multipliable` statement for the product is tracked in\n`QOU/MassEndomorphism/PhiQConvergence.lean`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.additive_wedderburn_recurrence_matches_std_rep_trace","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.additive_wedderburn_recurrence_matches_std_rep_trace","kind":"theorem","line":150,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L150-L166","doc":"**Recurrence = seminormal transfer-matrix action (derived).**\n\nThe script's `(a, b)`-recurrence step is exactly the action of the\nHoefsmit seminormal `2×2` transfer matrix\n`!![c·H_A + d, c; c, d]` on the row vector `(a, b)`: the first\ncomponent is `a·(c·H_A + d) + b·c` and the second is `a·c + b·d`.\nThis is the genuine computation underlying gap 2 — the matrix\n*entries* are the seminormal-form input `[hoefsmit1974] §3`; the\n*identity* between the script recurrence and the matrix action is\nderived here by `ring`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.wedderburnStep","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.wedderburnStep","kind":"def","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L141-L146","doc":"One step of the `additive_wedderburn_volumes` state-machine\nrecurrence: from `(a, b)` and a crossing `(c, d)` with\n`H_A = q - q⁻¹`, produce\n`(a·c·H_A + a·d + b·c, a·c + b·d)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.q_0_inversion_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.q_0_inversion_unique","kind":"theorem","line":98,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L98-L120","doc":"The m_μ/m_e → q_0 inversion is uniquely solvable, conditional\non the substrate's M_ratio function being strictly monotone on\nthe window (1, 2) and hitting the CODATA target there. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.MRatioInversionMonotone.exists_q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.MRatioInversionMonotone.exists_q","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L94-L94","doc":"Existence: some q ∈ (1, 2) hits the CODATA value. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.MRatioInversionMonotone.mono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.MRatioInversionMonotone.mono","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L92-L92","doc":"Strict monotonicity on the substrate window (1, 2). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.MRatioInversionMonotone.M_ratio_QOU","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.MRatioInversionMonotone.M_ratio_QOU","kind":"def","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L90-L90","doc":"The QOU-predicted m_μ/m_e ratio as a function of q. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.MRatioInversionMonotone.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.MRatioInversionMonotone.mk","kind":"ctor","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L88-L88","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassComputationChain.MRatioInversionMonotone","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassComputationChain.MRatioInversionMonotone","kind":"class","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassComputationChainRoadmap.lean#L75-L94","doc":"The QOU mass-ratio inversion class (§3b-cond axiomatisation).\nGiven the substrate-defined `M_ratio_QOU : ℝ → ℝ`, the\ninversion is well-posed on the substrate window (1, 2) if the\nfunction is strictly monotone and surjects onto the value of\n`M_ratio_codata` (the latter is a one-point reachability claim).\n\nThe `noncomputable instance` is the conjectural witness; the\n`sorry` in any instance is understood as the conjectural\nwell-posedness of the substrate's M_ratio function, NOT a\nmissing proof of the uniqueness theorem below — which is a\npure consequence of the class hypotheses. ","signatureChunk":0}]}